      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Two hundred sixty-four general practice patients were treated with oxamethacin (100 mg b.i.d.) and 261 with indomethacin (50 mg b.i.d.) for a maximum period of two weeks. Both drugs showed equally good analgesic and anti-inflammatory effects. Thirty-six patients (14%) treated with oxamethacin and sixty patients (23%) treated with indomethacin reported side-effects. Among them, seventeen patients on oxamethacin (6%) and forty-tree patients on indomethacin (16.5%) reported gastro-intestinal disorders. Side-effects necessitated interruption of treatment in fourteen patients (5%) on oxamethacin and in thirty-three patients (13%) on indomethacin."},
